  - Budget truncated because `200000` was sized to 96px for a drawn `150,000.00` that needs 72.61px of content.
  - Progress sat 33px under the right-pinned Actions column because *other* columns were over-estimated from stored values, so the Tasks table was 1230px wide. Assignee got 240px for 171px, from a stringified lookup object.
  - The card's "percent stored 0.45" was a progress field stored as `45`; the defect was real either way.
- **Seat's own reading of the diff:**
  - The auto-width estimate now reads back what each auto-sized column's header and cells **drew**, in a layout effect. Where the page is laid out it uses the measured natural width plus the measured cell inset. Where it is not, it uses the drawn text length with the old 8px/char rule. The stored value is used only before the first draw.
  - The 80px floor, the 400px cap, the header-only masked column, explicit `width`, `fitContent` and user-dragged widths are unchanged.
  - No second formatter: the cell's own drawn text is the source, as the order required.
  - Right-pinned columns each stick at the measured width of the pinned columns after them, mirroring `measuredStickyLefts`, so an author's right pin beside Actions is no longer drawn under it.
- **Convergence, checked by the seat** (this is the core table, so the read must not feed itself):
  - each read compares a signature of the targets' column, text and cell classes, and skips the measurement when it is equal;
  - an equal measurement writes no state, so it costs no commit;
  - the `max-content` probe is set and restored inside one task, on elements with no React-managed `style`;
  - the MutationObserver watches text and nodes only, so the probe's attribute writes are invisible to it;
  - the ResizeObserver re-read is deduped by the same signature, and a read with no layout is not remembered;
  - reads are skipped while a cell is edited.

  Recorded, not blocking: the signature walk (`querySelectorAll` plus `textContent` over the drawn page) runs after every render of the table. It reads no layout, so it forces no reflow. A very large page pays it per render.
- **The dev's open question (a table wider than its container): A.** At scroll 0, a sticky pinned column overlays the scrolling column passing beneath it. That is what pinning means, and every cell is reachable, with none covered at the scroll end. The measured lists now fit, and stacked pins no longer cover each other. A separate pinned pane would be an unpulled layout rewrite.
